.Sh CAVEATS
Each gate is a search for markers.
It helps to find what it names and never shows that nothing else is
there: only a reconciled dependency inventory shows that foreign
material is excluded, and only the native build shows that the code
compiles under the flags.
.Pp
No gate preprocesses or parses.
Code in
.Ql #if 0 ,
a macro that expands to a banned call, a call through a function pointer,
a comment marker inside a string, a dialect chosen in a variable that is
set elsewhere, a non-base tool not named above, and a construct split
across a line continuation are not seen.
A banned name followed by
.Ql \&(
in a comment or string is reported.
.Pp
.Nm mk-lint
does not evaluate conditionals: a
.Cm check
target inside a false
.Ic .if
still counts.
It reads the strict flags only on the
.Va CFLAGS
line itself, not on its continuation lines, and does not see a
.Va CFLAGS
given on the
.Xr make 1
command line.
.Pp
The awk programs are not themselves judged for GNU awk extensions; a run
under the base
.Xr awk 1
is what shows they need none.
.Sh SECURITY CONSIDERATIONS
The gates read untrusted bytes: a returned tree is written by a seat and
is judged before anyone reviews it.
They protect the honesty of their result, not any secret.
They trust
.Xr sh 1 ,
.Xr awk 1 ,
.Xr find 1 ,
.Xr grep 1
and
.Xr sort 1
of the base system, and their own scripts.
They read only under
.Ar root
(and
.Pa /dev/null ) ,
write only to standard output and standard error, create no file, use no
network and read no clock.
Only base programs read the contents of a judged file; the shell handles
names alone, after
.Pa walk
has checked them against the name alphabet, and never runs text from the
tree as shell source.
Nobody can delegate authority over a gate; it holds none to delegate.
A gate that cannot complete its walk or a tool run reports UNKNOWN and
changes nothing.
.Pp
They are shell components, and STANDARDS section 5 asks that a shell
component be confined by a C99 launcher.
The kit has none yet, so nothing but the user's own permissions limits
what a compromised base tool could touch while a gate runs.
